At 17:45 -0400 on 31.05.2002, Christopher C. Chimelis wrote regarding Re: 
DS20L:
>On Fri, 31 May 2002, Maurice Hilarius wrote:
>
>>  We feel that any machines that REQUIRE a cold room should come with a
>>  prominent sign on them to that effect, and that any sales literature should
>>  point that out in big bold letters.
>
>Why a prominent sign?  Why is literature that accompanies these products
>not enough?  If a manual says "don't operate this outside of x temperature
>range", how many more places need that information be posted?

I think vendor can't say TOO MUCH IN LARGE BOLD TYPE FONTS what
has to be... but just put general information.

Actually, room temperature matters, but also internal air temp is more 
important.

Like David says, Compaq ES45 shut itself at 53c inside... I bet that means
on top of DIMM or Tsunami chip is much hotter.
I think DRAM's operating temp of the surface is 70c or 80c and room temp
is under 55c.

When first production of UP2000, we had a lot of problems with heat
killing Tsunami chips.  But API corrected it by changing air-flow of
CPU fan direction I guess.

>So, the point shouldn't be whether or not the computer as a whole can
>be reliable at extreme temperatures (for the record, the CS20 itself can
>definitely run firmware tests reliably at temperatures in excess of those
>where I saw the DIMM failures), but whether each of the component parts
>can operate reliably at those temperatures.

Group of small component parts makes 1 machine, so that's right.
And memory/chipset is 1 part but I think power supply is another big weak
point of heat(condenser that is).  Some power supply uses 85c limit parts
instead of 105c.

>If you had a hard drive that
>failed above 20C due to heat stress, how could that be covered in the
>computer's documentation or be the basis for an argument like we've seen
>about the CS20?

If it failed above 20c, that's mfg's problem.  But like Maurice says,
after problem, company announcing that this part wasn't meant to be used
24/7....oops.

PS: I never experienced test chambre, but we had problems, submitted
for chambre test, had no errors, returned to us, same problem happened.
Of course, we had 100V(to be exact slightly above, not below) ..
Also, when we test firmware or some stress testing by TOOL APPS it
generally shows GOOD result, but when we test user level applications
it started to fail(different type of stress to CPU/memory???)
Generally, pre-shipping burn-in test with Gaussian98 prevent further
problem at valuable customer's site.

again, my $0.02.




