>With regards to your message at 01:08 PM 6/1/02, Ted Goodridge, Jr. Where 
>you stated:
>>My $0.02,
>>
>>As for 21264's not being affordable, I would take a close look at the
>>UP1500 (I hope I got that right)  For about 3k you can get something
>>that will smoke any Intel / AMD system out there (if what I have read
>>and have been told is true.)
>True, it is a fast machine, and affordable.
>But, frankly, is there any future for Alpha? I see the talent slowly moving 
>on to other platforms with a future.
>
>
>And with no future platform support ( heck not even CURRENT platform 
>support) under Linux..

HP/Compaq has made commitments of Red Hat Linux on Alpha on a PDF
somewhere on their website for many years to come.  The Alpha
IIRC, is roadmapped out to 2008 or 2010 roughly, so I don't see
any reason for the panic of the disappearing Alpha, or
disappearing Alpha distro.  HP seems quite committed from what I
read on their web site.

Keeping packages building on Alpha is not a complex task.  Only
keeping the toolchain, kernel, and glibc and a few other things
running on it is where the most time and effort is spent.  Since 
Hammer will be the new boy in town, I think it is safe to say 
that Alpha will benefit greatly from the increased 64bit 
cleanliness likely to result when 64bit Linux on Hammer is 
mainstream.

Regardless, Alpha will live on for some years to come, and
panicing off to another distro is unnecessary IMHO.  There is no 
reason whatsoever to believe Red Hat Linux will not appear on 
Alpha in the future - either officially - or unofficially.

