See one of the problems is that people forget that the alpha range 
covers quite a wide variety of CPU's and internals.
7.1 was created with the ev4-ev6 CPU range in mind. the PC164 is 
unfortunately one of those beasties that didn't like to conform to the 
natural progression of the CPU and motherboard design.

Even with 7.2 the primary focus was ev6 and above, BUT, a big effort has 
been made to be ev4, ev45, ev5, ev56 capable. Personally I just plain 
don't have time or access to every model to test everything, however, 
Jay has been incredibly helpful in finding older model problems and 
providing solutions that I've tried to intergrate seemlessly. By all 
accounts it would seem to have gone exceptionally well.

> > Perhaps you (and others so obsessed with 7.2's release date) would be more 
> content if instead of calling it RedHat 7.2, they were to call it RedHat 
> 7.4. That way it wouldn't be late (whatever that means) and you'd be ahead 
> of the x86 folks.....anyone??


I really all the distro's hadn't gone down the version number release 
naming convention. I personally never liked that scheme. Really 7.2alpha 
is i386 7.2 + all errata - some intel specific packages + some borrowed 
bits from 7.3 where I deemed it necessary for technical improvement 
reasons. Humm maybe thats a little too much information.


> P.S. FWIW, you state above you have a PC164, last I knew RedHat was 
> supporing only EV6(+) systems in the 7.x series. 
>

EV6+ though ev56 was directly supported as well (Hey, I've an sx164, did 
you think I was going to ignore it?? 8) )

