On Sun, 2 Jun 2002, Maurice Hilarius wrote:

>Thanks Mike. It is pleasant to hear a response like this giving
>us some insight as to what is happening. Up to now the silence
>on this topic has been worrisome.
>
>We have a great interest in 7.2-axp, as do many of our customers.

As do I.  ;o)

>When it has become delayed to the point where the next release is already 
>shipping on the x86 platform, and that 7.2 is shipping for the IA64 
>platform, even though there is essentially NO USER BASE on that platform, 
>it becomes acutely discomforting at times.

Well, I don't know the specific details, and this in no way is 
any form of official statement on it, but the way that I see it, 
is:

Our main product is the x86 version, which is self sustaining.  
Ports to other architectures, I'm not sure fit that model 
however.  In order for us to justify doing a port to another 
architecture and commit the resources to doing so, and seeing it 
through to completion, we need to look at how many resources, how 
many developers, etc will be required, and determine if the 
amount of effort, and cost to us to do the work, will result in a 
net profit off the results.  If we stand to lose money by doing a 
port to some architecture, then I believe we are unlikely to do 
the port.

However, if there are companies/hardware vendors out there that
want a port done, and can justify paying us to do the port, and
cover our development costs, etc. and they will see returns on
what they've spent - then there is a business model for such a
port for it to happen.

So, chances are for any architecture which is not financially 
self sustaining, I would presume some kind of contract with a 
hardware vendor is what ultimately makes the product happen.  If 
that vendor sells 20 machines a year and paying us to do the port 
makes them money, then there's a good chance it will happen.

So the number of machines out there for which there is or may be
a market for us to sell our product IMHO isn't too relevant below 
a certain threshold.  It then requires hardware vendor contracts.

At least that's how I have always envisioned it.  However, I'm
not a financial expert, so I don't really know the real details
of how the decisions/contracts etc. for a given port are done.  
I'll leave that up to people in marketing, and financial, etc. to 
work out, and head back to my code cave.  ;o)


>I hope that the issues with the ES4x machines is something that can be 
>resolved soon, so that the rest of the Alpha world may enjoy the use and 
>benefit of this product.

If everyone had ES4x's, then everyone would be much happier we're 
fixing the problems.  ;o)

ES45 sure is a nice machine.  I definitely recommend everyone get 
one.  ;o)


-- 
