I have two Alpha systems that have been running Debian/Alpha for years.  One 
is a PC164 500 Mhz, the other an LX164 533 Mhz; both purchased from Microway 
delivered with Redhat.  One of the first things I did was convert them to 
Debian because I have been running Debian on my Intel boxes for years prior 
to that.

They are both running Debian/Testing (or Woody) which is soon to be released 
as stable once security maintenance for all archs are finished.  Machine #1 
has an uptime of the PC164 is presently 34 days while machine #2 is 61 days.  
The only reason they go down is a manual reboot or a power outage in which 
the UPS reboots it or to upgrade the kernel.  Both machines are running 
custom 2.4.18 kernels.  Both machines run kde as the window manager.  The 
primary mission of both machines is number crunching using Compaq FORTRAN 
compiler and web/file service running Apache, nfs, samba, etc.  These 
machines being several years old still outperform in floating point 
performance any PC in our division.  One new IBM RS6K power 3 workstation we 
recently purchased is approximately twice the performance at several times 
the cost.

Now I ask, what more do you want ?

-- 
