>   From: "Mike A. Harris" <mharris@redhat.com>
>   Date: Sat, 1 Jun 2002 17:05:41 -0400 (EDT)
>
>   Regardless, Alpha will live on for some years to come, and
>   panicing off to another distro is unnecessary IMHO.  There is no 
>   reason whatsoever to believe Red Hat Linux will not appear on 
>   Alpha in the future - either officially - or unofficially.

Just don't hold your breath while waiting for new versions or updates.

Remember, there is "Support" and then there is "Support." 

Your expectations for "Support" probably represent about 10% of HPQ's 
definition of "Support." [note: that 10% is the consistant output of Focus
groups over the past many years.]  Hell, even "yesterday" (ie, before the
Alpha sale announcement), many of us didn't think that much of Q's support
for Alpha. Our expectations were far higher than Q ever even tried to
deliver, let alone did deliver.  Don't get me wrong, high expectactions are
a good thing, it keeps the vendors hopping... but the only people who
consistantly "approve" of vendor support are those folks with very well
defined "black box" type applications. Or put another way, the only people
who consistantly rate (any) vendor's support highly are people who have
very low expectations to begin with.

Alpha will definately live on in those instances and for those applications
where one can simply "set it and forget it."  Just as the PDP and VAX have
for lo these many, many, many years!

Compaq's road map for Alpha and the OSs which run on it, is wonderful and
rosey out through 2010 ...IF they can continue to exist until 2004 when the
first glimmer of an IPF based system and merged OS emerges. In my opinion,
HPQ does NOT have until 2004 to get IPF systems out the door. They are
going to have to do it before 2003 is half over, ie a year from now, and
even at that, I'm being gracious according to some lot of folks.

Don't forget what happened to Sequent when they announced all of their
future systems would be Itanium based. New customer sales STOPPED.
HPQ is a much bigger company, and they can "afford" to pour money down a 
rat-hole for a much longer period of time. However, today, Alpha is the
only thing that made Q a profitable company. If Alpha sales take a nose
dive, I doubt that HPQ can sell enough printers to make up the defecit.

In my opinon, the HP Compaq merger was actually a good fit. Selling
off Alpha however, was like the lawyer who performed surgery on himself
just so he could save on the surgeons bill -- It was done for strickly
financial reasons, not based on techincal merit.

-- 
