(heh, guess who's also a certified AIX specialist..)
The power2 had the problem that the binutil toolchain couldn't be made 
open src and that effectively stoped any chance of the model 6150 from 
having linux ported to it (its the big 6 chips on a CPU card model)

The PPC 60Xe chips were slated to have semi decent floating point 
performance but only in certain areas like barrel rolling so depending 
on how clever you were about assembling the code you could get decent 
performance out of them.

AIX is ok as far as it goes. It beats pretty well every unix out there 
when it comes to disk and data management. Linux has a very long way to 
go before it could claim to be able to compete with AIX in that field. 
Admittidly AIX is horrible in other areas 8) It really comes down to the 
best fit what you need a computer to do. If its data integrety on disk 
then AIX/rs6000s is a good choice. Computational computing is Alphas 
niche and general computing/gaming is intel turf (ok thats horribly 
simplified)

