I'm still following up myself as I dig deeper and deeper into
Alcor/CIA troubles...

I finally succeded into booting 2.4.19-pre10 by simply disabling the
DAC window, even if memory fits into the window. This is ugly, but it
allows me to boot the system (see included patch). I'd really like
someone with proper knowledge to give it a look.

And here comes yet another problem. I use this machine (yes, this very
same AS500/333) from a serial console. While the kernel boots,
everything is fine, and messages are comming normally on the serial
console.

Once it reaches user-mode to run init, any output through /dev/ttyS0
is awfully slow (it takes about 15 minutes to display the whole boot
sequence, instead of 20 seconds with 2.2).

One interresting thing to notice is that kernel messages are still
displayed at normal speed (for exemple, I'm seeing the "Adding
swap..." message from the kernel long before seeing the 'Activating
swap' message from the init scripts...

Another symptom is that *no serial interrupts* gets reported :

maz@sahara:~$ cat /proc/interrupts 
  1:          0          XT-PIC   keyboard
  2:          0          XT-PIC   cascade
  4:          0          XT-PIC   serial  <-- why 0 ???
  8:    2200468             RTC  +timer
 20:       5147           ALCOR   sym53c8xx
 29:      50043           ALCOR   eth0
 47:          0           ALCOR   isa-cascade
ERR:          0
maz@sahara:~$ 

So it looks like a case of bad ISA irq routing or something like that.
Once booted, the machine works fine from the network (I'm running many
ssh sessions to this system without problem).

The Alcor quest continues... ;-)

        M.


--=-=-=
Content-Type: text/x-patch
Content-Disposition: attachment; filename=patch.cia

--- usr/src/linux-2.4.18-0.26a/arch/alpha/kernel/core_cia.c	Sun Oct 21 19:30:58 2001
+++ linux/arch/alpha/kernel/core_cia.c	Sun Jun  9 10:47:29 2002
@@ -716,7 +716,7 @@
 	   which is better than before.  However, due to assumptions made
 	   elsewhere, we should not claim that we support DAC unless that
 	   4GB covers all of physical memory.  */
-	if (is_pyxis || max_low_pfn > (0x100000000 >> PAGE_SHIFT)) {
+	if (1 || is_pyxis || max_low_pfn > (0x100000000 >> PAGE_SHIFT)) {
 		*(vip)CIA_IOC_PCI_W3_BASE = 0;
 	} else {
 		*(vip)CIA_IOC_PCI_W3_BASE = 0x00000000 | 1 | 8;

