On Mon, Jun 10, 2002 at 09:57:54AM -0400, Mike A. Harris wrote:
> On Mon, 10 Jun 2002, Robert M. Riches Jr. wrote:
> 
> >I have a similar situation with X on a UP2000 and an
> >Oxygen VX1, with a bit of a twist.  At random intervals,
> >often when opening a new web browser window, either the
> >monitor reports sync rates out of range, the monitor
> >reports no video input, or the colors rotate (what should
> >be red turns blue, blue turns green, green turns red, or
> >maybe it's the other way around).  Switching to a text
> >virtual console and back usually cures it (until next
> >time).
> 
> That sounds more like a 2D acceleration bug to me.  Try disabling 
> accel with Option "noaccel" and see if it goes away.  If it does, 
> re-enable accel and then experiment one at a time with the 
> XaaNoxxxx options documented on the XF86Config manpage.  If you 
> can find one XaaNo option which makes the problem go away, it may 
> be possible to solve the problem.  That assumes it is a 2D accel 
> issue of course.

Well, they could certainly be bugs, but they could also be artifacts
of living with the Alpha architecture from user space.

On VX1 (and other Permedia chips), it is expensive, WRT instructions
needed to be issued, to update various parts of the chipset, primarily
the color palette and the hardware cursor.

On x86, the code to do such can be protected from interruption by, get
this, turning off interrupts! From user level! Under Tru64, the kernel
has graphics driver components that perform the operations that need
to be "atomic", such as those mentioned above.

Not so on Alpha. This opens a "window of opportunity" for another
operation to confuse the instruction stream to the VX1 with references
to other registers, etc.

Often, changing to software cursor can help get rid of some of these
instances, but they still do happen every now and then even with that.

But by all means, try Mike's suggestion, it could very well lead to
identifying the area(s) of code that could possibly be tweaked to cut
down on the glitches...

 --Jay++

