Content-Type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
Content-Disposition: attachment; filename=README-Alpha-HW

Hardware-related Notes
======================

Graphics Options
================

TGA and TGA2
------------

These controllers are supported by the XFree86 4.1.0 release drivers, but
GUI install with them under RedHat 7.2 does not work. The best work-around
for this limitation is to force "text" mode install. This can be done by
choosing the aboot.conf line that includes the "text" argument, or by adding
"text" to the manually-entered boot command line.

After installation is complete, correct operation of these servers depends
on adding a "BusID" option to the /etc/X11/XF86Config-4 file's Device
Section.  Note the device specification of the TGA/TGA2 in the output of
"lspci" (eg "00:08.0 Display controller: Digital 21030 TGA"), and use that
information to fill in the correct option line (eg BusID "PCI:0:8:0").

OpenGL support
--------------

OpenGL (3D) support in XFree86 4.1.0 that works on Alpha includes ATI
Rage128 and Radeon, and 3Dfx Voodoo Banshee and Voodoo3 2000 and 3000.
These seem to work well on all the officially supported platforms:
DS10/DS20/ES40/ES45. Other platforms that also seem to work well include the
other EV6-based boxes (XP1000, UP2000, and UP1000/1100, though the latter
two "Nautilus" machines don't seem to work with AGP versions of those cards,
just PCI versions), Older platforms that also seem to do well are: MIATA,
LX164, SX164, and UX164. Other platforms can be problematic, mostly because
the BIOS emulation built into their consoles isn't up to the task of running
the BIOS code on the more recent cards.

OpenGL programs that may be installed include many of the xscreensaver
demos, such as "gears" and "sproingies", and the Mesa demos; in addition,
both "tuxracer" and "Chromium B.S.U." are available on the 3rd CD; if the
install is not an "everything" install, you can choose those two by
selecting via the specific packages menus.



Legacy Platforms
================

Although not officially supported by the Red Hat 7.2 Alpha release, most of
the older Alpha platforms will install and run reasonably well, dependent
on peripheral options and available memory.

Less than 128 MB of memory will feel quite cramped, especially if using the
GNOME or KDE environments, or large applications such as Mozilla or
Netscape.

Reasonable installs (with respect to packages chosen) will run from 1.5 GB
to 2.5 GB in size, with an "everything" install running to 3.8 GB or
more. Server installs, eschewing software and kernel development (ie the
compilers and development libraries) as well as XFree86, can come in less
than 1 GB.


SRM-based machines
------------------

The following older platforms running SRM console were tested and found to
perform reasonably well with the 2.4.9-based kernels: JENSEN, AVANTI family
(AS250 tested), CABRIOLET (tested), EB164 family (EB164/PC164/LX164/SX164
tested), ALCOR family (AS500 tested), NORITAKE family (AS1000A/AS800
tested), RAWHIDE family (AS1200/AS4000/AS4100 tested), SABLE family (AS2100
4/275 and 5/250 tested), MIATA (tested), XP1000 (tested), Nautilus (UP1100
tested).

Some SRM platforms that may be problematic: Noname/UDB/AlphaBook1.


MILO-based machines
-------------------

Since the generic kernel used to perform the install is based on 2.4.9, the
MILO used must be capable of booting a kernel NOT configured to use the
"legacy start address". In most cases, this means a MILO based on the 2.2
kernel sources.

In addition, the "initial RAMdisk" facility is also used extensively, not
only for starting the install, but in normal post-install booting as well,
as the kernels deployed are all very modular in nature. This also requires
a 2.2-based MILO.

The following older platforms running ARC/AlphaBIOS console were tested and
found to perform reasonably well with the 2.4.9-based kernels: XLT family
(XL366 tested), Ruffian (UX164 tested), EB164 family (PC164 tested).

See the MILO Appendix in the Installation Guide on the documentation CD for
more MILO-related information: where to find them, how to boot them, and
how to install them.


Platform/Controller Miscellany
==============================

DAC960 (Mylex RAID controllers)
------

This driver has been patched to support correct operation in configurations
with more than 2GB of memory, such as might be found in DS20/ES40/ES45.

QLogic (SCSI)
------

The stock 2.4.9 kernel QLogic driver has been replaced with the ported BSD
version found on www.feral.com. It performs better and handles most if not
all configurations which have been problematic for the stock driver (such as
those with a tape drive or CDROM on the same SCSI cable with hard drives).

JENSEN
------

Know the correct parameters for loading the needed drivers, especially "io="
and "irq=".

TULIP (21040/21041/21140/21142/21143 Ethernet chips, also third parties)
-----

If the default "tulip" driver does not work during a network-based install,
use the "noprobe" boot option to allow choosing the "de4x5" driver instead.
But be careful, as then you also need to choose the other drivers to handle,
for example, the SCSI disks ("noprobe" means you have to do ALL that are
needed).


--zYM0uCDKw75PZbzx--




