This is an upgrade install. the initrd-2.4.9-32.img should be in /boot
if it's not then you need to create it using mkinitrd

Alternatively, you may be lucky and the initrd won't be needed (think of 
it as a repository for kernel modules that are needed for booting)
so the boot line

	b /boot/vmlinuz-2.4.9-32 root=/dev/sdb1

May simply just work
Otherwise you'll have to boot from the CD in rescue mode, and then at 
the # prompt
chroot /mnt/sysimage
mount -a
mkinitrd -f -v /boot/initrd-2.4.9-32.img 2.4.9-32
sync
reboot

Theres a complex technical reason why that will fails on a 7.0 -> 7.2 
upgrade install

Phil
