On Sun, Jun 16, 2002 at 09:20:20PM -0500, Earl Truss wrote:
> I've been unable to get anything going with any of the suggestions up to
> this point -- so I tried doing a full install instead of an upgrade.
> During the installation, it loads the ncr53c8xx and DAC960 drivers and
> everything appears to work fine through the install,

If you can do that than this means that you can boot your system at
least into a ramdisk and you have a full access to your hard disk.  This
is enough to fix whatever is to be fixed.  But are you sure that
there is really anything to fix with a possible exception of your
boot command which may be trying to use a wrong name for a kernel
and/or wrong initial ramdisk?

If you really know that this is not the case then boot your distribution
CD, pick up "Update an existing system" and that you want to pick up
yourself packages to update and when you will get that list it means
that everything is mounted.  On the second console, IIRC, (ctrl+F2 or
alt+ctrl+F2) you have a shell.  By typing 'cat /proc/mounts' there you
can find out where everything is mounted, 'chroot' to the top of your
file system on a hard disk and follow from there.  Before that check
with 'lsmod' which modules were really loaded to get you there.

> When I try the first suggestion or the succeding one to
> preload the drivers ..
> 
> > mkinitrd -f -v --preload sd_mod --preload aic7xxx --preload sym53c8xx
> > --preload jbd --preload ext3 /boot/initrd-2.4.9-32.img 2.4.9-32
> 
> it says "No module ncr53c8xx found for kernel 2.4.9-32". so now I'm
> further behind than I was with the upgrade.

Where are you typing these commands if you, presumably, cannot boot
your system?  The above makes sense when you got to your hard disk
as otherwise where these modules are supposed to be coming from
and where you are writing your initrd?

"No module ncr53c8xx" means that you have /etc/modules.conf which
says, among other things:

alias scsi_hostadapter ncr53c8xx

but no such module around.

You may edit 'ncr53c8xx' to 'sym53c8xx', or comment out this line, or
use '--omit-scsi-modules' option, or - the most radical - rename
/etc/modules.conf to something harmless and load all modules you need
explicitely using '--with' option.  Do not forget to check that
you have in /etc/aboot.conf a boot command which is using a kernel
you want to boot and initrd you want load.

BTW - manpage for initrd says that a syntax is not '--preload module'
but '--preload=module' and '--with=module' (although the other form
will be also accepted - at least with the current version :-).
'mknitrd' is really a shell script and it is not hard to find out
how it works and/or edit a private version which you need to adjust
it a bit.

  Michal




