Don't hold your breath... the inverse is more likely to be true -- Alpha
will be a memory by 2004, just like the VAX.

The latest DH Brown Unix survey just relased, has moved Tru64 from top of
the heap where they were in the same survey last year to dead last.
HP-UX is now top dog.
http://story.news.yahoo.com/news?tmpl=story&u=/cn/20020605/tc_cn/932330 
Note that DH Brown surveys are not known to be unbiased by who pays for
them. 

HPQ has also just announced that HP-UX 11i (Itanium) will have most of the
features found on the PARC version, including PARC emulation.
http://story.news.yahoo.com/news?tmpl=story&u=/zd/20020610/tc_zd/934443

And HPQ has announced "greater than expected savings" in the merger as well
as accelerated job eliminations.

Other than contractual obligations, I don't forsee "customer demand" for
the Alpha being something that HPQ is going to be looking to promote. 
It conflicts with too many other corporate goals.  (And there are not
enough Alpha customers spending enough dollars to make a difference...
support revenue doesn't count here, just NEW hardware sales and licenses.) 

We stopped buying Alphas two weeks after their end was announced. 
(Long before the HP announcement.) And while we have not made any plans to
actively replace our existing 30 Alphas, they are NOT being replaced with
Alphas. We typically buy 3 year extended warranties and replace the
machines as the warranties expire, about 10 a year on a roughly 3 year
cycle. [Sadly, we've been buying SPARCs, which I think is a bad idea, but
that's what "everybody else" thinks is preferrable to Itanium.] 

And don't forget -- "ongoing support" does NOT mean new hardware OR
software... just continued fixing of old bugs. The services organization is
geared to "supporting" legacy systems, the rest of the organization is
geared to creating "new and improved."

