First, I upgraded two LX164s with no problems. One is my main workstation,
the 2d is a fairly idle server with one student doing web development.
Both work well. Good job.

Upgrading a DP 264 server has been a different matter. The RAID controller
(ICP Vortex) has never been supported by RH, but I've sorted out how to
work around that fairly gracefully.

The NIC, however, is failing almost (but not quite) completely. I don't
have the make/model of the card on any paperwork (just 10/100 fast PCI
ethernet). It is 21143 based ( from dmesg and tulip-diag report). I use
the de4x5 driver.  The tulip driver failed the one time I tried. 100Mb/s,
FD must be forced (as on all alpha boxes here Linux and Tru64), although
the switch settings downstream have been 100/Full.  The card worked that
way under 7.1. (Forcing is from SRM boot_osflags, but it works on the LXs 
from aboot.conf.)

Symptoms: 

	1. ping <gateway> yeilds many "no path" errors followed by
	packet returns.

	2. Ping local machine: approxmately 30 "no path" errors, then
	packet returns

	3. Ping more remote (< 300 ft) machine: about the same.

	4. Ping half a continent away: this actually worked right away.

	5. All attempts to access the machine remotely time out.

100Mb and link lights are on. The full duplex light is off. Activity light 
flashes in what appears an appropriate manner.

The networking campus administrator reset the switch this machine is
plugged into to half duplex. Same behavior (fewer tests).

Kernels attempted: 

	1. Release SMP kernel 2.4.9-31. boots to maintenance mode,
	since it does not find the RAID module. The de4x5 module is
	loaded. network FAILS, as above.

	2. Custom kernel with gdth and de4x5 compiled in (not modules).
	FAILS. Most tests on this kernel.
	Version: 2.4.19-pre5-vm33, recompiled for ext3 support.
	This has run for a few weeks on this machine.

	Tried a cold (!) boot. Same results.

I migrated file systems on the system disk to ext3. Raid array remains
ext2 at this point. Custom kernel has ext3.

One peculiarity showed up after the upgrade. The hostname.domainname were
dropped and I had to run netconfig (or whatever) by hand (and have to do
some hand editing to complete the configuration -- no secondary resolver).
Since I can (inconsistently) get reach other hosts, and since the route
table looks correct, the basics have to be correct.

Any suggestions? (I'm planning to scour the kernel configuation carefully.
As a last resort, I'll try another NIC - 3COM 59X. File system migrate
waits till this is sorted out.)

-- 
Mark Hansel
