Hi,

I'm a first time Alpha user (just bought an 1000A 5/333 from an auction
last Sunday), and so far I've managed to install RH7.2 on my system.
Kudos for RH/Compaq team, the installation went smoothly with only few
minor problems, which I would like to ask to dear members of this list.

1. When installing..
     * first I partition the disk (SCSI, Seagate Barracuda) using
       AlphaBIOS.
     * Then after booting to RH CD from SRM, disk druid or fdisk
       complained that the disk layout is incorrect. And I couldn't
       create a new partition either.
       But I could delete partitions (and I did delete some).
     * Then I booted the JumpStart CD. Everything was fine, and
       I can repartition the disk.
     * Then I booted the machine again. But this time, I activated
       the SCSI BIOS Emulation in AlphaBIOS.
     * Then after switching to SRM and reboot the machine, I started
       the RH CD. This time, I _was_ able to repartition the disk.
     * Everything went smoothly after this. Installing ~1.8 GB
       of RPMs took about 30 mins.

     Can anybody explain what was happening here (or give pointer
     to docs) ?

2. I tested the harddisk performance with hdparm -t, but the
     result is somewhat very less than I expected. The result
     for hdparm -t is about 1.5 MB/s, while hdparm -T is about
     55 MB/s. Compared to my IDE machine (AMD 450Mhz running
     RH-7.2) that gives me 14 MB/s buffered read and 68 MB/s
     buffer-cache read, this is quite dissapointing for me.

     *Note that at this point, I still enable SCSI BIOS Emulation
      in AlphaBIOS menu, although I don't know what it actually
      does.

     Anybody can suggest on how to improve the HD performance?

My system is (as far as I can remember):
  - AlphaServer 1000A 5/333
  - Everything is from RedHat 7.2 CD
  - QLogic ISP 102 (?) internal SCSI controller.
  - 2 X 4 GB SCSI disks (DE something, Seagate Barracuda ST13471)
    1 X 8 GB SCSI (Compaq, ?)
  - 256 MB memory
  - Latest AlphaBIOS and SRM (5.70-8).

Sorry if I don't give much clue in this email, I can get some more clues 
once I get back home (well, that if anybody cares to answer.. :))

many thanks,
Benny





