Thanks :) The refresher on kernel config was very informative :)  I have
never used mkinitrd before... and I probably am missing some module
needed on bootup (now that you mention it, its probably the bsd
partition driver;)  I'll do some reading on mkinitrd tonight.

For the most part I'm not going to change the config of the stock kernel
(at least for now) as to assist the RedHat team tracking down bugs (if
there are any.)  I am still *AMAZED* at how things that I could never
get to work with the unpatched kernels work beautifully.  It seems the
conventional wisdom about unpatched kernels and non-x86 archs is true.

I'm most interested in getting the kernel with ev56 enhancements, and
with your suggestions, I'm going to give it another whirl this weekend
(nasty long shift tomorrow...I really hope the economy improves soon ;)
)

Ted

> -----Original Message-----
> From: Michal Jaegermann [mailto:michal@ellpspace.math.ualberta.ca]
> Sent: Thursday, June 20, 2002 11:55 AM
> To: Ted Goodridge, Jr
> Subject: Re: recompiling the stock 7.2 kernel
> 
> On Thu, Jun 20, 2002 at 10:49:00AM -0500, Ted Goodridge, Jr wrote:
> >
> >  When I try to boot the new kernel, I get
> > 'init not found' and a kernel panic.
> 
> That most likely means that you forgot to include a support for
> an access to a disk you are using and/or type of partitioning employed
> on your boot disk and/or a filesystem on '/'.
> 
> If any of those are loaded as modules then you should use
> 'mkinitrd' to create a corresponding 'initrd' image and add
> 'initrd=....' to your boot command.
> 
> > Is there a RedHat specific
> > way of doing things?
> 
> No, but a split of responsibilities may vary a bit.  In your custom
> kernel you do not need to load as modules things you _know_ that you
> will need always.  A distribution packager trying to cover the widest
> possible range of configurations does not have such comfort (unless
> willing to baloon a kernel in size and to waste unswappable kernel
> memory on things which on a particular configuration will be never
> used).
> 
> > Each distro has their own way making it work
> > better,
> 
> Not really if you are not talking about minor details.  A Linux boot
> sequence is predetermined by a kernel and you do not have that much
> leeway until 'init' is running.
> 
>   Michal





