On Tuesday 25 June 2002 12:30 pm, Dan C Green wrote:
> I am running RH 7.2 on an ev6 (Tsunami).  I have built a 2.4.18 kernel.
>  Everything seems to work great except that every morning I come in to
> find a kernel panic.  I do not get a register/stack dump.  The message
> is: Interupt handler not synching. Killed interupt handler Aiee (or some
> approximate message).  That's about the jist of it, there doesn't seem
> to be any real information (to me anyway).  I am wondering if there is
> something that I am not doing in my kernel build that is causing this.
>  Anybody have any ideas?

hmm..
 Difficult to say exactly, but if you find it every morning then I guess 
what's causing it is one of the overnight cron jobs your systems probably 
running: take a look in /etc/cron.daily, and execute each of the scripts in 
there in turn: one of them will probably be causing the panic (if not, look 
for anything else that runs at nighttime and not during the day).
 It that gets nowhere (and I think it might be something to do with network 
filesystems, as the stock 2.4.18 had a problem with codepages in smbfs that 
caused my alpha to barf periodically), then rebuild the kernel with the Magic 
SysRQ calls enabled: you might get a bit more information from this. Post the 
output from it back here (see /usr/src/linux/Documentation/sysrq.txt for 
details of what it can provide), and I'm sure someone will be able to suggest 
a possible cause.

cheers
john




