True in general - the OP would have to deal with that. 
I have no DVD writer, so I do not know their capacity, 
and "DAT" capacity also differs with drive and cartridge type.

> 
> That is why I suggest grabbing the data to a directory first, making sure
> the size is not too big, and dealing with that first.
> THEN use CDRECORD to write the data to the DVD

I deleted your original post, so I can not check: 
Did you propose to make an ISO file system and copy that? 
I do not know whether one of the several ISO limits restricts 
the file size or not, but definitely that would not be a simple 
"copy" as originally asked. Depending on the DAT contents, 
that might matter.

Some platforms provide "bootable tapes" (AIX does - I enjoy that), 
putting that into an ISO FS would be fatal. 
(Direct tape copy to DVD would not work there either, I admit.)

Also, disk space becomes an issue with the copy.

Well, I think the main issue is the OP should use "cdrecord" 
and adapt the input (tape copy or ISO FS) to the situation.

