On Thu, 27 Jun 2002, Danilo Godec wrote:
> I have an 164SX machine with 533 MHz processor. Here is the /proc/cpuinfo:

> What I can't figure out is what kind of 'arch' this is: alpha, alphaev5,
> alphaev56, ... ? Can someone help me out?

any '164 of clock speed greater than 333MHz or something like that is an
ev56, as far as instruction set is concerned.  this is a bit of a
generalization, but close enough for most purposes.

> What are those 'ev*', anyway?

ev stands for "Electric Vlassic".  it's a DEC in-joke.  you can look up
the electric vlassic experiement paper online.  one of the DEC research
centers did that old experiment where you put electricity through a pickle
and it glows.  then they tried it with other things like bok choy :)

you'll find some people who claim that ev stands for "Extended Vax" but
that's not nearly so much fun.  the real truth is that like AXP, it
doesn't really stand for anything.  it's just a unique identifier.

 -ross




