Okay, let me ask a slightly different question.

Does anyone know of a JVM that supports recent classes (1.3.x, 1.4.x) 
that will run on Alpha Linux?

I see Compaq's release at http://www.compaq.com/java/download/ but the 
.tgz file just has an RPM inside it.  I run a custom Linux install and 
have built everything from source.  I don't want to go through the 
trouble of building the RPM tools (which have dependancies on older 
Berkley DB releases) to just install this one thing.  It looks like I'd 
also have trouble with libots and libcpml.

So I figure my options are to see if someone would run alien on the RPMs 
that I need, so I can untar them and install the stuff manually.  Or I 
could find a compatible JVM.  I think Kaffe is the only one that comes 
close to what I need, but it doesn't seem to work on the Alpha at this 
point.  I would actually rather compile the JVM on my own to keep in 
style with the rest of my machine.  I've looked at compiling the source 
Sun released, but you need a working javac, kinda a catch-22.  I may 
eventually cross compile Sun's release on an x86, but that also falls 
into the too-much-work-department, as I don't have any x-compile tools 
built right now.

So what does everyone else that isn't running an RPM based distro do for 
Java?

Thanks again,
Chris




